From ddbab107af99f3dbbc2d9f2924bcad304f875f2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hemant Kumar <heman10x@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 21:51:42 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] local: cache streamed experts, exact-sized pools, 0.0% ->
 28.3% hits

Slots now persist across calls, layers and tokens, keyed by (tensor, expert),
with LRU eviction and a guard that never evicts a slot the current fetch batch is
already using.

THE FIRST ATTEMPT MEASURED EXACTLY ZERO HITS AND THE ARITHMETIC SAYS IT HAD TO.
Slots were allocated in two size classes, small for gate/up and large for down,
which cut the ~50% waste of one uniform size down to 26-28%. But the number that
decides this is not the waste percentage, it is what one lap costs IN SLOTS:

  one token's expert data            1.649 GiB = 1,689 MB
  one token in two-pool slots        258x2.23 + 258x2.23 + 258x4.52 = 2,316 MB
  arena provided                     2,048 MB

2,048 < 2,316, so the arena held less than one full cycle of a strictly cyclic
access pattern, and LRU evicted every entry immediately before its turn came
round again. Result: 0/17478 hits, with zero failed reads, zero fallbacks and
correct output - every health signal green while the feature did nothing.

Fixed with one pool per distinct stride (1,638,400 / 2,162,688 / 3,211,264 /
4,456,448), rounded only to the sector rather than to 64 KB, each sized in
proportion to the bytes per token that stride accounts for so the pools exhaust
together rather than one throttling the rest. Padding falls from 26-28% to about
0.25%, so a lap costs ~1,693 MB instead of 2,316.

Measured, same cap and prompt, n=16:

  two-pool 2,048 MB    17,478 reads   37.29 GiB   126.6 s    0.0% hits
  exact    2,600 MB    12,522 reads   26.65 GiB   114.1 s   28.3% hits

10.58 GiB served from RAM. Bytes off disk down 28.5%, wall time down 10%, on top
of the 23% the streaming reader had already taken from the mmap path.

The init line now reports how many laps the arena holds and warns below 1.05,
because this failure is silent and looks exactly like success.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
